{
  "id": "kjv",
  "name": "the King James Bible",
  "title": "The King James Bible",
  "tagline": "And, and, and: the cadence of the Authorized Version",
  "epigraph": {
    "text": "In the beginning God created the heaven and the earth.",
    "cite": "Genesis 1:1"
  },
  "advisor": "King James Advisor",
  "short": "the King James Bible",
  "whole": "the whole of the King James Bible",
  "years": "1611",
  "action": "King James it up",
  "citeStyle": "verse",
  "pronouns": {
    "subject": "it",
    "object": "it",
    "possessive": "its",
    "possessivePronoun": "its"
  },
  "epistolary": [
    "romans",
    "1corinthians",
    "2corinthians",
    "galatians",
    "ephesians",
    "philippians",
    "colossians",
    "1thessalonians",
    "2thessalonians",
    "1timothy",
    "2timothy",
    "titus",
    "philemon",
    "hebrews",
    "james",
    "1peter",
    "2peter",
    "1john",
    "2john",
    "3john",
    "jude"
  ],
  "persona": "The Authorized Version of the Bible (1611), made by some fifty translators for King James I and read aloud in English churches for four hundred years: plain, old, concrete words; clause after clause joined by “and”; thou and thee, -eth and -est; the parallel halves of Hebrew verse; a grave cadence meant for the ear.",
  "echoes": [
    "A child who left home and squandered everything comes back, and is welcomed: the prodigal son, Luke 15.",
    "A stranger helps someone everyone else passed by: the good Samaritan, Luke 10.",
    "Someone runs away from what they were asked to do, and it goes badly: Jonah.",
    "A friend refuses to leave: “whither thou goest, I will go”, Ruth 1.",
    "Brothers who are jealous of the favourite: Joseph and his brothers, Genesis 37.",
    "A time for every thing: Ecclesiastes 3."
  ],
  "manner": [
    "Go the whole way: the passage should read as a chapter of the King James Bible, not as the writer’s page in old spelling. A rewrite that keeps their sentences and swaps a few words has failed them.",
    "Lay clause beside clause with “and”, and open most sentences with “And”; divide the halves of a sentence with a colon or a semicolon. Subordinate clauses are rare, and “because” is seldom where “for” will do.",
    "Address one person as thou, thee, thy and thine, and many as ye, with the verbs that go with them (thou art, thou hast, thou knowest); give he and she the verb in -eth (he goeth, she saith).",
    "Turn the story with “And it came to pass”; call attention with “behold”; use unto, upon, thereof, wherefore, whither and the like where the Bible would.",
    "Where the passage invites it, say a thing twice in parallel halves, the second echoing the first in other words.",
    "Give feeling by its sign, gravely and plainly: “and I wept”, “and his countenance fell”, “and my heart was heavy within me”. No irony, no knowing aside: the voice does not wink.",
    "Speech is given without quotation marks, brought in plainly (“And he said unto me,”) and given in full; a question keeps its question mark."
  ],
  "craftNotes": [
    "Keep what the writer named, in its order, and how much (“a whole pint”, “more snacks”). The narrator stays in the writer’s person: a first-person passage stays first person (“And I said…”, “and I went”).",
    "A modern thing keeps the writer’s own name for it, set in the Bible’s sentence (“the charger of my phone”, “a canoe”, “the truck”): it is never turned into another thing, and never made a riddle. That is the joke and the truth of it. A riddle (“sweet food eaten cold” for ice cream, “the place where they sell drink” for a coffee shop) is worse than the modern word: say ice cream, say coffee shop.",
    "Render what the writer meant, not the nearest phrase that sounds like scripture: “really cool” is liking and admiration, not beauty; “gross” is distaste, not sin.",
    "Mind the words whose Bible sense a reader hears: to lie with or know a person is carnal; meat is any food; a charger is a dish; conversation is conduct; let is hinder; prevent is go before; quick is alive. Never use one where that sense would be heard: a bunkmate sleepeth beside thee, he does not lie with thee.",
    "Add nothing religious the writer did not imply: no God, no prayer, no blessing, no sin, no moral, unless the passage has it. The voice is the Bible’s; the story stays the writer’s.",
    "Short, old, concrete words; a Latinate abstract word only where the Bible itself has one (mercy, iniquity, righteousness, salvation).",
    "A letter keeps the form of an epistle, without its piety: greeting at the head (“Unto my father and my mother, greeting.”), and at the foot the writer’s own hand (“The salutation of me Jake with mine own hand.”, after 2 Thessalonians 3:17), and a postscript as an afterword (“Moreover, …”)."
  ],
  "moves": [
    {
      "id": "and",
      "name": "Clauses laid side by side with “and”",
      "does": "Clause after clause joined by “and”, sentence after sentence opened by it, where English now would subordinate: “And God said, Let there be light: and there was light.”",
      "pattern": "^\\W*and\\b|[:;,]\\s+and\\b[^.]*[:;,]\\s+and\\b",
      "register": "any"
    },
    {
      "id": "cametopass",
      "name": "And it came to pass",
      "does": "A new turn of the story opened by “And it came to pass”, or closed by “and it was so”.",
      "pattern": "\\b(it came to pass|it was so|it shall come to pass)\\b",
      "register": "any"
    },
    {
      "id": "thou",
      "name": "Thou and thee, -eth and -est",
      "does": "The singular you as thou, thee, thy, thine, the plural as ye; the verb in -est after thou and in -eth for he and she: “thou knowest”, “he goeth”.",
      "pattern": "\\b(thou|thee|thy|thine|ye|hath|doth|saith|shalt|wilt|hast|dost|art\\s+thou|\\w{3,}eth)\\b",
      "register": "any"
    },
    {
      "id": "behold",
      "name": "Behold, lo, verily",
      "does": "Attention called with “behold” or “lo”, an oath with “verily”.",
      "pattern": "\\b(behold|lo|verily)\\b",
      "register": "any"
    },
    {
      "id": "unto",
      "name": "Unto, thereof, wherefore",
      "does": "The old prepositions and adverbs: unto, upon, thereof, therein, wherefore, whither, thence, hither, yea, nay.",
      "pattern": "\\b(unto|thereof|therein|wherefore|whither|thence|hither|yea|nay|whereupon|thereupon)\\b",
      "register": "any"
    },
    {
      "id": "saidunto",
      "name": "Speech brought in by “said unto”",
      "does": "Speech introduced plainly, without quotation marks, by “And he said unto them,” and given in full.",
      "pattern": "\\b(said|spake|answered|saith|cried)\\b[^,:]{0,30}\\b(unto|to)\\s+\\w+[,:]",
      "register": "any"
    },
    {
      "id": "parallel",
      "name": "Parallelism",
      "does": "One thought said twice, the second half echoing the first in other words, the halves divided by a colon or semicolon: “Pride goeth before destruction, and an haughty spirit before a fall.”",
      "pattern": "^[^:;]{12,}[:;]\\s*(and|but|for|yet)?\\s*[^:;]{12,}$",
      "register": "any"
    },
    {
      "id": "of",
      "name": "The genitive with “of”",
      "does": "Possession and kind given by “of”: “the house of the LORD”, “the children of Israel”, “the day of his wrath”, not “the LORD’s house”.",
      "pattern": "\\bthe\\s+\\w+\\s+of\\s+(the\\s+|his\\s+|my\\s+|thy\\s+)?\\w+",
      "register": "any"
    }
  ],
